          "text": "CUDA graphs allow you to capture a sequence of kernel launches and their dependencies into a single graph object, then replay that graph with a single launch call. The primary speedup comes from **eliminating per-launch CPU overhead**. When you launch kernels eagerly, the CPU must perform a series of operations for each kernel: validate arguments, set up the command buffer, push it to the GPU driver, and synchronize with the GPU\u2019s command queue. This overhead\u2014often 5\u201310 microseconds per kernel\u2014becomes significant when you have thousands of small kernels (e.g., in deep learning or scientific simulations). With a graph, you capture this sequence once, and replaying it submits the entire graph as a single unit, amortizing the CPU cost across all kernels. The GPU also benefits because the graph is pre-validated and pre-optimized, so the driver can schedule work more efficiently, reducing gaps between kernels and improving GPU utilization.\n\nAnother key advantage is **explicit dependency management**. In eager launches, the driver must infer dependencies from memory reads/writes, which can lead to conservative serialization or extra synchronization barriers. CUDA graphs let you specify dependencies explicitly (via `cudaGraphAddDependencies`), so the scheduler can overlap independent kernels that",
